Keep the IOKit root nub out of the type zones, and print each step of InitIOKit (judyp bring-up).

The previous round bracketed PE_lockdown_iokit, StartIOServiceMatching and the work loop allocation, and
none of those lines appeared: the console still ends on "lockdown_mode: lockdown mode in nvram is off",
which bsd/kern/lockdown_mode.c prints from its startup function, well before IOKit. The panel's stage row
named PE_lockdown_iokit because the last frame the sampler caught was PE_init_iokit+0x388, and
PE_lockdown_iokit is inlined into PE_init_iokit immediately after the two calls that follow that address:

  0bf4d154  bl _InitIOKit
  0bf4d158  bl _ConfigureIOKit
  0bf4d1ac  bl _printf                 <- the trace the previous round added
  0bf4d1b0  bl _zalloc_iokit_lockdown
  0bf4d1bc  bl _printf
  0bf4d1c0  bl _StartIOKitMatching

So the boot never reaches PE_lockdown_iokit: it stops inside InitIOKit, one call earlier than the previous
round assumed. The same photograph says why nothing progressed afterwards -- the panel froze at t=8s with
DAIF=200, interrupts masked, which is the "IRQ-off hard lock" its own heading warns about -- and it also
rules out a shortage, with 1188120 pages free.

InitIOKit (iokit/Kernel/IOStartIOKit.cpp) has exactly one step that allocates: gRootNub = new
IOPlatformExpertDevice. IOPlatformExpertDevice::nw is a tail call into OSObject_typed_operator_new, which
allocates through kalloc_type_impl_external, that is through a kalloc_type zone. A ticket lock taken there
and never released is precisely a silent, panic-free, IRQ-off lock.

Two changes, one round:

  1. OSObject_typed_operator_new stops using the type zone and takes every OSObject from kheap_alloc --
     the path the plain OSObject::operator new has always used and the path operator delete frees to. If
     the type zone is the fault, this boot continues past the root nub; if it does not, the type zones are
     not implicated and the next round can leave them alone.

  2. InitIOKit reports after each of its steps and around the three calls that build the root nub, so a
     boot that still stops names the step rather than the function. This is also what makes the first
     change readable: the zalloc trace added last round is gated on native_wl_trace, and the gate now
     opens at the root nub instead of at startIOServiceMatching, so the zone names print for the window
     that matters.

The prints are diagnostics and the gate closes again right after attach(); only the allocation change is
behavioural.

diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/IOPlatformExpert.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/IOPlatformExpert.cpp
index d0149a6..30a8b62 100644
--- a/iokit/Kernel/IOPlatformExpert.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/IOPlatformExpert.cpp
@@ -2086,15 +2086,24 @@ IOPlatformExpertDevice::init(void *dtRoot)
 	return true;
 }
 
+extern "C" int native_wl_trace; /* NATIVE_SM: read by osfmk/kern/zalloc.c */
+
 bool
 IOPlatformExpertDevice::startIOServiceMatching(void)
 {
+	native_wl_trace = 1;
+	IOLog("NATIVE_SM: workLoop enter\n");
 	workLoop = IOWorkLoop::workLoop();
+	IOLog("NATIVE_SM: workLoop=%p\n", workLoop);
 	if (!workLoop) {
+		native_wl_trace = 0;
 		return false;
 	}
 
+	IOLog("NATIVE_SM: registerService enter\n");
 	registerService();
+	IOLog("NATIVE_SM: registerService done\n");
+	native_wl_trace = 0;
 
 	return true;
 }
